drugs come in different sizes from small  simple molecules like aspirin to giant  complex biological drugs like monoclonal  antibodies in recent years biological  drugs or biologics have revolutionized  treatments for cancers metabolic  diseases and inflammatory disorders in  2016 roughly a quarter of all global  drug sales were biologics but there's a  problem  these drugs are incredibly difficult and  time-consuming to make so they're often  prohibitively expensive that may be  about to change the patents on many  biologics will soon expire which means  that competitors can try to make cheaper  copycat versions called biosimilars  making a generic version of a small  molecule drug isn't too difficult  chemists can reverse engineer the  structure and use familiar synthesis  techniques to create an exact copy  but biologics are much more challenging  making an exact copy is impossible  because of their size and complexity and  the original manufacturers have no  incentive to share their assembly plant  so the biosimilar developers have to  start from scratch first developers need  to reproduce the basic structure as best  they can  based on careful analysis of batches of  products and published data then they  need to replicate the three-dimensional  drug with functional parts this is such  a complex process that scientists rely  on cultures of living cells to assemble  the drug for them by ceding control to  this black box system of living cells  the exact synthesis pathway remains  hidden and is impossible to fully  control another issue is that small  differences in these live cultures like  temperature pH nutrients and so on can  cause chemical modifications which can  affect the biologics function and safety  so the team must experiment to find the  right conditions to make the drug in  their own lab once it's been made the  new drug must undergo stringent tests to  check that its chemistry and function  are similar to the originates drug for  example it needs to undergo functional  tests to make sure it binds to its  target then the new drug needs to be  compared to the originator drug  clinical trials to make sure it works as  well as its predecessor and doesn't set  off an immune reaction or have any other  unexpected effects in the body  no virus similar will ever be identical  to the originated drug but modest  chemical differences are acceptable if  the drug passes these rigorous tests and  clinical trials this whole process can  take more than ten years but it's an  effort worth making if it means that  some blockbuster biologic drugs become  more affordable and accessible for  patients in the coming years  some say that biosimilars could reduce  global healthcare spending by a hundred  and sixty billion dollars over the next  five years
 

